     "date": "2026-07-30T10:31:55Z"
    },
    "message": "perf(analytics): close sqlite connections, offload admin queries, reclaim pruned pages\n\nAnalytics and request-log storage fixes verified by measurement against the\nlive 143 MB request database (4,635 rows / 138.9M body chars).\n\n- request_log: add a closing `_connection()` helper and use it on all read and\n  maintenance paths. `sqlite3.Connection.__exit__` only commits/rolls back, and\n  connections are GC-tracked rather than reference-counted, so scope exit leaked\n  file descriptors until the next collection (measured: 172 -> 327 OS handles\n  across a 200-read burst, recovered only by an explicit gc.collect()).\n- request_log: give the writer thread one persistent connection instead of\n  reconnecting per flush batch, which re-ran the WAL/synchronous pragmas on\n  every write. This path fires on ordinary proxy traffic, not just analytics.\n- request_log: cap bodies in `enqueue()` rather than at flush time. Queued\n  records previously held uncapped input, so a backlog could retain far more\n  than the persisted 50k-char per-row limit.\n- request_log: enable `auto_vacuum=INCREMENTAL` and run `incremental_vacuum`\n  after a prune that removed rows. Pruned pages previously stayed on the\n  freelist, so the file size only ever ratcheted upward.\n- request_log: project list queries over explicit metadata columns with\n  SQL-side `substr` previews and body lengths instead of `SELECT *`, so full\n  bodies are no longer loaded into Python just to be truncated.\n- request_log: memoize `stats()` for 5s to collapse duplicate dashboard bursts.\n- admin_routes, admin_websearch_routes: run all 8 analytics endpoints' SQLite\n  work via `asyncio.to_thread`. They were `async def` calling sync sqlite\n  inline; FastAPI only offloads plain `def` handlers, so a stats query blocked\n  the event loop for its full duration (measured 3.2-3.7s with zero concurrent\n  health pings completing).\n\nAdds regression tests for cap-before-enqueue, connection closure under\ndisabled GC, auto_vacuum mode, file size across insert/prune cycles, and stats\ncache behavior.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
